[2026-05-29 21:54:59] [INFO] [UNKNOWN] Input: {"session_id":"982b9502-87ae-44d6-bfc3-ef26db9a536e","transcript_path":"C:\\Users\\Administrator\\.claude\\projects\\D--CodeDevelopment-CodeProject-claude-hk\\982b9502-87ae-44d6-bfc3-ef26db9a536e.jsonl","cwd":"D:\\CodeDevelopment\\CodeProject\\claude-hk","permission_mode":"plan","effort":{"level":"medium"},"hook_event_name":"PostToolBatch","tool_calls":[{"tool_name":"Agent","tool_input":{"description":"Explore backend.sh and skill scripts","subagent_type":"Explore","prompt":"I need to understand the current degradation/fallback strategy implementation in the claude-hk project. Specifically:\n\n1. Read `.claude/skills/backend.sh` — the shared backend API call helper. Understand what functions it provides and how it handles backend unavailability.\n2. Read `.claude/skills/003-4-issue-claim/scripts/03UserPromptSubmit.sh` — this is the issue claim skill, which should have backend integration with fallback.\n3. Read `.claude/skills/003-5-issue-fix/scripts/03UserPromptSubmit.sh` — the issue fix skill.\n4. Read `.claude/skills/003-9-issue-review/scripts/03UserPromptSubmit.sh` — the review skill.\n\nSearch breadth: medium. Focus on understanding how `_backend_available`, `_call_backend`, and degradation logic works across these skill scripts."},"tool_use_id":"call_596ac84d882d492a9d692c1a","tool_response":[{"type":"text","text":"\nBased on my analysis of the files, here's the current degradation/fallback strategy implementation in the claude-hk project:\n\n## Backend Integration Architecture\n\nThe project uses a centralized `backend.sh` helper that provides common functions for backend API integration with graceful degradation.\n\n### Core Backend Functions (`backend.sh`)\n\n1. **`_backend_available()`** - Health check function\n   - Checks if backend URL is configured and reachable\n   - Uses 2-second timeout for quick availability check\n   - Returns true only if both URL exists and `/health` endpoint responds\n\n2. **`_call_backend()`** - Main API caller with fallback\n   - Takes endpoint and JSON data parameters\n   - 5-second timeout for API calls\n   - **Key degradation logic**: Silently returns empty string (exit code 1) if:\n     - No backend URL is configured\n     - CURL command fails or returns no response\n   - All errors are suppressed (2>/dev/null) - truly silent failures\n\n3. **`update_issue_status()`** - Status update helper\n   - Used by D2-D6 skills (claim, fix, review)\n   - Follows the same pattern: check backend URL → get session ID → get repo → call API\n   - **Important**: Uses `> /dev/null 2>&1` - completely silent on failures\n\n### Degradation Strategy Analysis\n\n#### 1. **Issue Claim (003-4)**\n```bash\n# In filter_claimed_issues():\n_load_backend_url\n[ -z \"$BACKEND_URL\" ] && { echo \"$issues_json\"; return; }\n```\n- **Fallback**: If backend unavailable, returns original issues list\n- **Impact**: Users might see issues already claimed by other agents\n\n#### 2. **Issue Fix (003-5)**\n```bash\n# Line 31: update_issue_status \"$ISSUE_NUM\" \"fixing\"\n```\n- **No explicit fallback check** - relies on `update_issue_status` being silent\n- **Behavior**: Completely silent if backend fails\n\n#### 3. **Issue Review (003-9)**\n```bash\n# Lines 41-48: Multiple status updates\nupdate_issue_status \"$ISSUE_NUM\" \"reviewing\"\nupdate_issue_status \"$ISSUE_NUM\" \"merged\"  # or \"rejected\"\n```\n- **No explicit fallback check** - relies on `update_issue_status` being silent\n- **Behavior**: Completely silent if backend fails\n\n### Key Design Patterns\n\n1. **Configuration-driven**: Backend URL loaded from `.claude/backend.conf`\n2. **Session-aware**: All operations require a session ID\n3. **Repository-aware**: All operations need the current repo name\n4. **Silent by Default**: All backend failures result in no output\n5. **Graceful Degradation**: Skills continue to work without backend\n\n### Current Issues with the Strategy\n\n1. **Overly Silent**: No logging or indication when backend calls fail\n2. **No Retry Logic**: Immediate fallback on first failure\n3. **No User Feedback**: Users have no idea if status updates succeeded\n4. **Race Condition Risk**: In issue claiming, if backend is slow, multiple agents might claim same issue\n\n### Suggested Improvements\n\n1. **Add logging**: Debug-level logs when backend calls fail\n2. **User feedback**: Indicate when backend is unavailable\n3. **Retry mechanism**: Exponential backoff for transient failures\n4. **Circuit breaker**: Track failure rate and temporarily disable backend calls\n\nThe current implementation prioritizes availability over consistency, which is appropriate for a degradation strategy but could benefit from better observability."}]},{"tool_name":"Agent","tool_input":{"description":"Explore backend Go code and tests","subagent_type":"Explore","prompt":"I need to understand the current Go backend implementation and existing tests in claude_tap_plus. Specifically:\n\n1. Read the backend API layer: `claude_tap_plus/internal/backend/api/` — look at router.go, issue_handler.go, session_handler.go, request.go, response.go\n2. Read the backend service layer: `claude_tap_plus/internal/backend/service/` — look at issue_service.go, session_service.go\n3. Read the backend store layer: `claude_tap_plus/internal/backend/store/` — look at store.go, issue_store.go, session_store.go\n4. Read existing tests: `claude_tap_plus/tests/backend/issue_api_test.go` and `claude_tap_plus/tests/integration/backend_skill_flow_test.go`\n5. Check if there's a `cleanup_service.go` in the service directory\n\nSearch breadth: very thorough. I need to understand all the existing backend code and test patterns to plan the degradation strategy and test verification work."},"tool_use_id":"call_249b56ae5ced4ce9aed58f33","tool_response":[{"type":"text","text":"\n## Summary of claude_tap_plus Backend Implementation\n\nBased on my thorough exploration of the codebase, here's a comprehensive overview of the Go backend implementation and test patterns:\n\n### **Backend Architecture**\n\nThe backend follows a clean architecture pattern with three layers:\n\n#### **1. API Layer** (`internal/backend/api/`)\n- **router.go**: Sets up HTTP routes\n  - `/health` - Health check endpoint\n  - `/api/issue/check` - Check issue status (creates idle records for new issues)\n  - `/api/issue/claim` - Claim an issue (with optimistic locking)\n  - `/api/issue/release` - Release a single issue\n  - `/api/issue/release-session` - Release all issues for a session\n  - `/api/issue/status` - Update issue status\n\n- **issue_handler.go**: Handles all issue-related HTTP requests\n  - Validates request payloads\n  - Calls service layer\n  - Returns standardized JSON responses\n\n- **session_handler.go**: Currently empty (not implemented)\n\n- **request.go**: Defines request DTOs for all endpoints\n\n- **response.go**: Defines response DTOs and helper functions\n\n- **health_handler.go**: Simple health check endpoint\n\n#### **2. Service Layer** (`internal/backend/service/`)\n- **issue_service.go**: Thin wrapper around store layer\n  - Implements business logic validation\n  - Handles optimistic locking claims\n  - Delegates to store layer for database operations\n\n- **session_service.go**: Empty file (not implemented)\n\n- **cleanup_service.go**: Empty file (not implemented)\n\n#### **3. Store Layer** (`internal/backend/store/`)\n- **store.go**: Contains interface definitions and SQLite implementation\n- **issue_store.go**: Core issue management logic with:\n  - Auto-creation of idle records for new issues\n  - Optimistic locking for claim operations\n  - Batch operations for session-wide releases\n  - Protection of terminal states (merged/rejected)\n- **sqlite.go**: SQLite store factory with WAL mode enabled\n- **migrations.go**: Database schema setup with tables for:\n  - machines\n  - projects\n  - sessions\n  - issue_claims\n\n#### **4. Domain Layer** (`internal/backend/domain/`)\n- **issue.go**: Defines IssueStatus enum and IssueClaim struct\n- **session.go**: Defines SessionStatus enum and Session struct\n\n### **Issue State Machine**\n\nThe backend implements a comprehensive issue lifecycle:\n\n```\nidle → claimed → fixing → ready-for-pr → pr-created → testing → reviewing → merged/rejected\n```\n\nKey states:\n- **Terminal states**: `merged`, `rejected` (cannot be modified or released)\n- **Working states**: `idle`, `claimed`, `fixing`, `ready-for-pr`, `pr-created`, `testing`, `reviewing`\n- **Auto-release**: All working states except `reviewing` are auto-released on session end\n\n### **Test Patterns**\n\nThe existing tests demonstrate:\n\n#### **1. Unit Tests** (`tests/backend/issue_api_test.go`)\n- Comprehensive test coverage for all API endpoints\n- Test helpers for setup, HTTP requests, and assertions\n- Tests verify:\n  - Input validation\n  - State transitions\n  - Error handling\n  - Concurrency scenarios\n  - Full lifecycle workflows\n\n#### **2. Integration Tests** (`tests/integration/backend_skill_flow_test.go`)\n- Tests simulate real skill workflows (003-4 through 003-9 skills)\n- Tests verify:\n  - Session management integration\n  - End-to-end issue lifecycle\n  - Skill-specific state transitions\n  - Session cleanup behavior\n  - Cross-session isolation\n\n### **Key Features**\n\n1. **Optimistic Locking**: Claims use UPDATE with status='idle' condition for atomic operations\n2. **Auto-creation**: New issues automatically get idle records on first check\n3. **Batch Operations**: Efficient session-wide issue releases\n4. **State Protection**: Terminal states cannot be modified or released\n5. **Error Handling**: Consistent error responses with standardized format\n6. **Clean Architecture**: Clear separation between API, service, and store layers\n\n### **What's Missing**\n\n1. **Session management**: Only schema exists, no actual session CRUD operations\n2. **Cleanup service**: Placeholder file not implemented\n3. **Error handling**: Limited to HTTP layer, no domain-specific errors\n4. **Metrics/Monitoring**: No observability features\n5. **Configuration**: Hard-coded database paths, no runtime config\n\n### **Test Quality**\n\nThe tests are well-structured and comprehensive, covering:\n- Happy paths and edge cases\n- Error scenarios and validation\n- Concurrent operations and race conditions\n- End-to-end workflows\n- State transitions and persistence\n\nThis implementation provides a solid foundation for issue management with clear patterns for extension and maintenance."}]},{"tool_name":"Agent","tool_input":{"description":"Explore hooks 01 and 29 integration","subagent_type":"Explore","prompt":"I need to understand how the hooks integrate with the backend for session lifecycle. Specifically:\n\n1. Read `.claude/hooks/01-session-start/base.sh` — should register session with backend on startup\n2. Read `.claude/hooks/29-session-end/base.sh` — should release issues and close session with backend\n3. Read `.claude/hooks/base.sh` — the shared infrastructure, understand dispatch_to_skill and other shared functions\n4. Check if there's a `.claude/backend.conf` file and what it contains\n5. Look at the requirement document `doc/otherDoc/2026-05-27/requirement-queue.md` item 12 about \"降级策略与测试验证\" to see if there's any detail about what exactly needs to be done\n\nSearch breadth: medium. Focus on understanding the hook-level backend integration and any degradation handling."},"tool_use_id":"call_bbd801cdecfd473683b09d36","tool_response":[{"type":"text","text":"\n## Summary: Hook-Level Backend Integration Analysis\n\nBased on my examination of the codebase, here's a comprehensive understanding of how the hooks integrate with the backend for session lifecycle:\n\n### 1. **SessionStart Hook (`.claude/hooks/01-session-start/base.sh`)**\n\n**Current State:**\n- Currently only performs environment setup (UTF-8, Python checks, directory creation)\n- **Missing**: Session registration to backend\n\n**Intended Implementation (from SR-2 requirement):**\n- Should register session via `register_session()` function\n- Detects if the claude-tap-plus proxy is active by checking `$HOME/.claude-tap-plus/.proxy.json`\n- If proxy is running, calls backend `/api/session/register`\n- Data sent: session_id, machine_id, os, project_slug, project_cwd, transcript_path, model, source\n\n**Key Design Points:**\n- No backend.conf needed - gets backend URL from proxy status\n- Silent failures if backend unreachable\n- Only runs when proxy is active\n\n### 2. **SessionEnd Hook (`.claude/hooks/29-session-end/base.sh`)**\n\n**Current State:**\n- ✅ **Implemented** - has `release_session_issues()` function\n- Calls `/api/issue/release-session` to release issues held by the session\n- Ignores non-merged/rejected issues\n- Silently fails if backend unreachable\n\n**Missing Implementation (from SR-3 requirement):**\n- Should also have `unregister_session()` function\n- Should call `/api/session/close` to mark session as closed\n- Data sent: session_id, reason\n\n### 3. **Shared Hook Infrastructure (`.claude/hooks/base.sh`)**\n\n**Key Functions:**\n- `json_get()` - extracts JSON values from stdin\n- `log()` - writes timestamped logs\n- `dispatch_to_skill()` - routes to skill-specific scripts based on event number\n- Hook input/output handling with JSON\n\n### 4. **Backend Configuration (`.claude/backend.conf`)**\n\n**Current State:**\n```bash\nBACKEND_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080\n```\n\n**Note**: The actual implementation now uses proxy state file instead of this config.\n\n### 5. **Degradation Strategy (Item 12)**\n\nFrom the requirement document and degradation strategy:\n\n**Core Principles:**\n1. **Backend is auxiliary, GitHub is primary** - backend only tracks state, doesn't replace GitHub ops\n2. **Silent failures** - backend failures shouldn't block user operations (except claim)\n3. **Config-driven** - no backend.conf means no backend calls\n\n**Degradation Behaviors:**\n| Scenario | Behavior |\n|----------|----------|\n| No backend.conf | Skip all backend calls |\n| Backend unreachable | Skip all backend calls |\n| `/api/issue/check` fails | Show all issues (no filtering) |\n| `/api/issue/claim` fails | **Block claim** (prevents multi-agent conflicts) |\n| `/api/issue/status` fails | Silent ignore, continue GitHub ops |\n| `/api/issue/release-session` fails | Silent ignore |\n\n**Testing Points from requirement:**\n- T12: Backend running then fails, subsequent ops shouldn't block\n- T13: After backend recovery, shouldn't conflict with degraded period ops\n- T14: Malformed backend.conf shouldn't crash system\n\n### Key Integration Points\n\n1. **Session Lifecycle Integration:**\n   - Start: Register session + ensure environment\n   - End: Unregister session + release issues\n   - Both hooks are designed to be non-blocking\n\n2. **Issue Lifecycle Integration:**\n   - SessionEnd hook automatically releases issues held by that session\n   - Only issues with status not 'merged' or 'rejected' are released\n   - Released issues become 'idle' and can be claimed by other agents\n\n3. **Safety Mechanisms:**\n   - Proxy state detection prevents calls when proxy isn't running\n   - Silent failures prevent blocking normal operation\n   - Claim is the only operation that blocks on backend failure (to prevent conflicts)\n\n4. **Current Implementation Status:**\n   - ✅ SessionEnd issue release is implemented\n   - ❌ SessionStart registration is not implemented\n   - ❌ SessionEnd unregistration is not implemented\n   - ❌ Proxy state detection logic is not implemented\n\nThe backend integration is designed to be graceful - if the backend is unavailable, the system degrades to its original GitHub-only behavior without affecting the user's ability to work."}]}]}
